\title[The blindness--sparsity dichotomy]{The Blindness--Sparsity Dichotomy for Plug-In Information Readings}
\subjclass[2020]{94A17, 62B10, 05A20, 62G09}
\keywords{plug-in mutual information, estimator bias, permutation test, collision fraction,
data-processing inequality, log-sum inequality, involution symmetry}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{23 September 2026}

\begin{abstract}
We study the empirical (``plug-in'') mutual information $\Ihat(\ell,c)$ between a categorical
label $\ell$ and a categorical \emph{view} $c$ on a finite sample of size $n$, in the regime
where the view is fine-grained relative to the sample --- the regime in which feature-leakage
screening is actually performed. We prove four things. First, a \emph{collision sandwich}:
with $C$ the number of samples sharing their view value with at least one other sample,
$H(\ell)-\frac{C}{n}\log_2|L| \le \Ihat(\ell,c)\le H(\ell)$, and for an injective view
($C=0$) the reading equals $H(\ell)$ identically, whatever the relationship between label and
view. Second, since a label permutation preserves both the label margin and the fiber sizes,
the \emph{entire} permutation null lies in the same band, so a shuffle test on a sufficiently
fine view provably has no resolution. Third, a data-processing inequality for the plug-in
functional on empirical tables, proved from the log-sum inequality with no distributional
hypothesis, shows that a finer ``hint'' view always reads at least as much as any coarsening
of it, with the gain bounded by the coarse view's collision term. Fourth, the
\emph{blindness--sparsity dichotomy}: on a \emph{swap-closed} sample --- one carrying a
fixed-point-free involution along which the view is constant and the label flips --- the
empirical table is an exact product table, so $\Ihat(\ell,c)=0$ exactly, while the collision
fraction is exactly $1$ and the sandwich is vacuous. Contrapositively, a sample whose view
fails to collide even once cannot be swap-closed: the two regimes are disjoint. Applied to a
factor-leakage screen, a flagged reading of $0.9663$ bits whose $200$-shuffle null had mean
$0.9648$ and standard deviation $0.0011$ is entirely sparse plug-in inflation.
\end{abstract}

