\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The screening problem}
A recurring computational-statistics task takes the following shape. One has a population of
structured objects, each carrying a hidden binary attribute $\ell$ that one hopes is
\emph{not} recoverable, and a family of computable summaries --- ``views'' --- of the same
objects. The question is whether any view leaks the attribute.

The motivating instance is factor-leakage screening. The objects are ordered pairs of distinct
primes $(p,q)$; the attribute $\ell$ is a which-factor bit (say, whether the first coordinate
is the smaller prime); the views are cheap functions of the pair or of the product $N=pq$ ---
the product residue $N \bmod m$ for a small modulus, hint views built from factor residues
such as a coded pair $(s,d)$ of a sum and a difference statistic, and joint labellings.

The standard instrument is the plug-in mutual information between label and view, calibrated
against a permutation null obtained by shuffling the labels. We show that in the regime where
these screens are typically run --- fine views, moderate samples --- both the statistic and
its calibration are structurally incapable of answering the question, and we determine exactly
when an alternative, exact-zero argument applies.

\subsection{The empirical observation}
A screening battery on prime pairs reported the following readings and $200$-shuffle nulls.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}
\toprule
view & observed (bits) & null mean & null sd & $z$\\
\midrule
product view $N \bmod 713$ & $0.0153$ & $0.0162$ & $0.0008$ & $-1.04$\\
$(s,d)$ hint view & $0.9663$ & $0.9648$ & $0.0011$ & $+1.36$\\
joint labels & $0.0011$ & $0.0008$ & $0.0002$ & $+1.44$\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Two features demand explanation. The hint view's $0.9663$ bits are nearly the full label
entropy, an order of magnitude above the other views, and were read as the hint view ``seeing
more''. Yet its permutation null reproduces the value to within $0.0015$ bits: the number
survives the complete destruction of label information by shuffling. Every view sits inside
its own null.

A controlled run on $n=3906$ ordered pairs of $63$ primes in $(50,2000)$ gave the
complementary picture: the product view realised $639$ distinct codes with observed reading
exactly $0.0000$ against null mean $0.1363$ (sd $0.0070$); the $(s,d)$ view realised exactly
$1953=n/2$ distinct codes --- one per unordered pair --- with observed $0.0000$ against null
mean $0.4990$; a residue view realised $3$ codes with observed $0.0000$ against null mean
$0.0004$. Here the nulls sit \emph{above} the data. The theory below accounts for all of it.

\section{Setting and definitions}

Throughout $n\ge1$ is the sample size, $L$ a finite label alphabet and $K$ a finite view
alphabet. A \emph{labelled sample} is a pair of functions
$\ell:\{1,\dots,n\}\to L$ and $c:\{1,\dots,n\}\to K$. Logarithms $\log_2$ are base two;
entropies and informations are in bits.

\begin{definition}[Counts]
For $a\in L$, $k\in K$ put
\[
N_{a,k}=\#\{i:\ell_i=a,\ c_i=k\},\qquad
f_k=\#\{i:c_i=k\},\qquad
m_a=\#\{i:\ell_i=a\}.
\]
Then $\sum_k N_{a,k}=m_a$, $\sum_a N_{a,k}=f_k$, $\sum_k f_k=n$ and $N_{a,k}\le f_k$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Empirical table and reading]
The \emph{view table} is $\phat(a,k)=N_{a,k}/n$, with margins $\phat_L(a)=m_a/n$ and
$\phat_K(k)=f_k/n$. The \emph{plug-in reading} is
\[
\Ihat(\ell,c)=\sum_{a\in L}\sum_{k\in K}\phat(a,k)\,
 \log_2\frac{\phat(a,k)}{\phat_L(a)\,\phat_K(k)},
\]
with terms having $\phat(a,k)=0$ omitted. For a mass function $r$ we write
$H(r)=-\sum_x r(x)\log_2 r(x)$, and $H(\ell)=H(a\mapsto m_a/n)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Collision count]
A sample index $i$ is \emph{colliding} if $f_{c_i}\ge2$. The \emph{collision count} is
\[
C(c)=\#\{i: f_{c_i}\ge2\}=\sum_{k:\,f_k\ge2} f_k,
\]
and $C(c)/n\in[0,1]$ is the \emph{collision fraction}. The view is injective on the sample iff
$C(c)=0$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Entropy cap]\label{lem:cap}
$\Ihat\le H(\phat_L)$, and the gap equals the conditional entropy of the label given the view:
\[
H(\phat_L)-\Ihat=\sum_k\sum_a-\,\phat(a,k)\log_2\frac{\phat(a,k)}{\phat_K(k)}\;\ge\;0 .
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Zero iff product]\label{lem:zero}
$\Ihat=0$ if and only if $\phat(a,k)=\phat_L(a)\phat_K(k)$ for all $a,k$; equivalently
$m_a f_k=N_{a,k}\,n$ for all $a,k$.
\end{lemma}

Both follow from Gibbs' inequality $\log x\le x-1$ applied cellwise.

\section{Fiber decomposition and the collision sandwich}

\begin{lemma}[Per-fiber gap]\label{lem:fibergap}
Fix $k$ with $f_k>0$ and let $\rho_k(a)=N_{a,k}/f_k$, a probability vector. Then
\[
\sum_a -\,\phat(a,k)\log_2\frac{\phat(a,k)}{\phat_K(k)}=\frac{f_k}{n}\,H(\rho_k).
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Write $\phat(a,k)=(f_k/n)\rho_k(a)$ and $\phat_K(k)=f_k/n$; the ratio inside the logarithm is
exactly $\rho_k(a)$ and the prefactor $f_k/n$ factors out of the sum.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Fiber decomposition]\label{prop:decomp}
\[
H(\ell)-\Ihat(\ell,c)=\sum_k \frac{f_k}{n}\,H(\rho_k).
\]
\end{proposition}

\begin{corollary}[Singletons are free]
If $f_k\le1$ then $\rho_k$ is a $0/1$ indicator, so $H(\rho_k)=0$. Only colliding fibers can
depress the reading below the label entropy.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[The collision sandwich]\label{thm:sandwich}
For every labelled sample with $n\ge1$ and every view $c$,
\[
H(\ell)-\frac{C(c)}{n}\log_2|L| \;\le\; \Ihat(\ell,c)\;\le\;H(\ell).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The upper bound is Lemma~\ref{lem:cap}. For the lower bound apply
Proposition~\ref{prop:decomp}, bound $H(\rho_k)\le\log_2|L|$ on colliding fibers, use
$H(\rho_k)=0$ on singletons and sum $\sum_{f_k\ge2}f_k=C(c)$.
\end{proof}

No assumption at all is made about the relationship between $\ell$ and $c$: the bound is an
identity about counting, not about dependence.

\begin{theorem}[The injective limit]\label{thm:injective}
If $C(c)=0$ then $\Ihat(\ell,c)=H(\ell)$ exactly, for every label function $\ell$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[The band is estimable]\label{thm:estimable}
Let $d$ be the number of distinct view values realised. Then $C(c)\le 2(n-d)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Split the $d$ realised values into $s$ singletons and $t$ colliding values, so $d=s+t$ and
$n=s+C(c)$. Since each colliding fiber has $f_k\ge2$, $C(c)\ge 2t$, whence
$d\le (n-C(c))+C(c)/2=n-C(c)/2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Tightness]
A constant view on $n\ge2$ samples has $C(c)=n$ and, for a balanced binary label, deficit
exactly $H(\ell)$. Hence the factor $C/n$ cannot be improved in general, and $\log_2|L|$ is
attained at a balanced label margin within a single fiber.
\end{proposition}

\section{The permutation null is trapped in the same band}

Let $\pi$ be a permutation of the indices and $\ell^\pi=\ell\circ\pi$.

\begin{lemma}[Permutation invariants]\label{lem:perminv}
$m_a(\ell^\pi)=m_a(\ell)$ for every $a$, and the view is untouched, so all $f_k$ and $C(c)$
are preserved.
\end{lemma}

Hence Theorem~\ref{thm:sandwich} applies to observed and surrogate with identical endpoints.

\begin{theorem}[Null band]\label{thm:nullband}
For every permutation $\pi$,
$\bigl|\Ihat(\ell^\pi,c)-\Ihat(\ell,c)\bigr|\le \frac{C(c)}{n}\log_2|L|$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[The null has no range]
For all permutations $\pi,\sigma$,
$\bigl|\Ihat(\ell^\pi,c)-\Ihat(\ell^\sigma,c)\bigr|\le \frac{C(c)}{n}\log_2|L|$, so the
observed value and the whole null lie in a common interval of that width.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[$z$-numerator bound]
\[
\Bigl|\,\Ihat(\ell,c)-\frac{1}{|\mathfrak S_n|}\sum_{\pi\in\mathfrak S_n}\Ihat(\ell^\pi,c)\,
\Bigr|\;\le\;\frac{C(c)}{n}\log_2|L| .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Write the difference as the average over $\pi$ of $\Ihat(\ell,c)-\Ihat(\ell^\pi,c)$ and apply
the triangle inequality termwise, using Theorem~\ref{thm:nullband}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The hinted view is blind]\label{thm:powerless}
If $\frac{C(c)}{n}\log_2|L|\le\delta$ then: every surrogate reading is within $\delta$ of the
observed reading; any two surrogates are within $\delta$ of each other; and the observed
reading is within $\delta$ of the null mean. A permutation test on such a view has no
resolution: the number it reports measures the view's sparsity, not the label's dependence on
the view.
\end{theorem}

\begin{remark}
On $n=3995$ ordered prime pairs with $3597$ distinct four-field codes,
Theorem~\ref{thm:estimable} certifies $C\le 2(3995-3597)=796$, so observed and all $200$
surrogates lie in $[H(\ell)-0.1993,\,H(\ell)]$. The measured observed-minus-null gap was
$0.0011$ bits, three orders of magnitude inside the worst-case guarantee.
\end{remark}

\section{Sharpening: the fiber band}

The alphabet factor is the worst case inside a fiber and is pessimistic for fine views.

\begin{theorem}[Support entropy bound]
For any probability mass function $r$ on a finite set, $H(r)\le\log_2 \#\supp(r)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Gibbs' inequality against the uniform measure on $\supp(r)$: the relative entropy is
nonnegative and the cross term evaluates to $\log_2\#\supp(r)$.
\end{proof}

Inside the fiber over $k$ at most $f_k$ labels occur, so $H(\rho_k)\le \log_2 f_k$.

\begin{theorem}[The fiber-log band]\label{thm:fiberlog}
\[
H(\ell)-\Ihat(\ell,c)\;\le\;\frac1n\sum_{k:\,f_k\ge2} f_k\log_2 f_k ,
\]
a bound with no reference to the label alphabet.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Pair-collision views: the one-bit window]\label{thm:onebit}
If $f_k\le2$ for all $k$ then $H(\ell)-\Ihat(\ell,c)\le C(c)/n\le1$, hence
$H(\ell)-1\le\Ihat(\ell,c)\le H(\ell)$, regardless of the label alphabet and of the
dependence.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
For $f_k=2$ one has $f_k\log_2 f_k=2=f_k$, so the fiber-log sum of
Theorem~\ref{thm:fiberlog} collapses to $\sum_{f_k\ge2}f_k=C(c)\le n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
Under the same hypothesis, every label-permuted surrogate reading is within one bit of the
observed reading.
\end{corollary}

\begin{remark}
In the controlled run the $(s,d)$ view realised exactly $1953=n/2$ codes on $n=3906$ ordered
pairs --- one per unordered pair --- so every fiber has size exactly two and
Theorem~\ref{thm:onebit} applies: a reported hint-view reading of $4.56$ bits against a label
entropy of $4.60$ is forced by fiber sizes, not measured. The product view $N\bmod 713$
realised $639$ codes with fibers up to size $16$, where Theorem~\ref{thm:fiberlog} gives
$2.766$ bits. The two bounds are genuinely incomparable (a single fiber of size $n$ gives
$\log_2 n$, worse than $\log_2|L|$ when $|L|<n$), so we keep both.
\end{remark}

\section{Refinement monotonicity: hint compounding is bias compounding}

A view $c$ is a \emph{coarsening} of $c'$ if $c=\varphi\circ c'$ for some
$\varphi:K'\to K$. On tables, coarsening aggregates columns:
$(\Phi_\varphi p)(a,k)=\sum_{k':\varphi(k')=k}p(a,k')$.

\begin{theorem}[Log-sum inequality]\label{thm:logsum}
For finite families of nonnegative reals $(a_i)_{i\in S}$, $(b_i)_{i\in S}$,
\[
\Bigl(\sum_i a_i\Bigr)\log\frac{\sum_i a_i}{\sum_i b_i}\;\le\;\sum_i a_i\log\frac{a_i}{b_i},
\]
with the usual conventions for vanishing terms.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
With $A=\sum a_i$, $B=\sum b_i$, apply $\log t\le t-1$ at $t=\frac{a_iB}{b_iA}$, multiply by
$a_i$ and sum; the resulting right-hand side cancels to $0$, giving
$\sum_i a_i\log\frac{a_iB}{b_iA}\ge0$, which rearranges to the claim. No normalisation of the
families is needed.
\end{proof}

Applying Theorem~\ref{thm:logsum} blockwise over each fiber $\varphi^{-1}(k)$, with
$a_{k'}=p(a,k')$ and $b_{k'}=p_L(a)p_K(k')$, yields:

\begin{theorem}[Refinement monotonicity]\label{thm:dpi}
For every nonnegative table $p$ on $L\times K'$ and every $\varphi:K'\to K$,
$\Ihat(\Phi_\varphi p)\le \Ihat(p)$. Aggregating columns can only decrease the plug-in
reading; no distributional assumption is used.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[Sample form]
For every sample, view $c'$ and post-processing $\varphi$,
$\Ihat(\ell,\varphi\circ c')\le\Ihat(\ell,c')$: adding a hint never lowers the reported
number.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Hint compounding is bias compounding]\label{thm:gain}
\[
0\;\le\;\Ihat(\ell,c')-\Ihat(\ell,\varphi\circ c')\;\le\;
\frac{C(\varphi\circ c')}{n}\log_2|L| .
\]
The gain from refining is nonnegative and no larger than the \emph{coarse} view's collision
term.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Nonnegativity is Theorem~\ref{thm:dpi}. For the upper bound, the fine reading is at most
$H(\ell)$ and the coarse reading is at least $H(\ell)-\frac{C(\varphi\circ c')}{n}\log_2|L|$
by Theorem~\ref{thm:sandwich}; subtract.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Strictness witness]
Take a balanced binary label and a view copying it exactly: the reading is $1$ bit. Coarsening
by the constant map to a one-point alphabet gives the single-column table
$(\tfrac12,\tfrac12)^{\mathsf T}$, whose reading is $0$. Refinement therefore strictly
increases the reading on a population whose underlying dependence has not changed.
\end{proposition}

\begin{remark}
The reported triple $0.0153$ (product), $0.9663$ (hint), $0.0011$ (joint labels) is not
monotone as printed. By Theorem~\ref{thm:dpi} this is impossible for genuinely nested views,
so the three views were not nested as claimed: the joint-label view is a relabelling, not a
refinement, of the product view. Where views are genuinely nested the ordering is forced and
carries no information.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}[Caveat]
Theorem~\ref{thm:gain} is informative only when the coarse view is itself near-injective. For
a genuinely coarse view the bound degrades to $H(\ell)$ --- the honest statement, since a
coarse view's deficit really can be filled by a refinement.
\end{remark}

\section{The blindness--sparsity dichotomy}

Sections 3--6 explain why a large positive reading on a fine view is not evidence. The
complementary half of the story delivers an exact zero: a symmetric readout on a swap-closed
population leaks exactly $0$ which-factor bits. Numerically the two halves appear to collide
--- exact reading $0$ versus sample reading $0.97$. They cannot, because their hypotheses are
mutually exclusive.

\begin{definition}[Swap-closed sample]\label{def:swap}
A labelled sample $(\ell,c)$ of size $n$ is \emph{swap-closed} if there is a permutation
$\sigma$ of $\{1,\dots,n\}$ with
\begin{enumerate}
\item $\sigma(\sigma(i))=i$ for all $i$ \quad(involution);
\item $\sigma(i)\ne i$ for all $i$ \quad(fixed-point-free);
\item $c(\sigma(i))=c(i)$ for all $i$ \quad(view symmetry);
\item $\ell(\sigma(i))\ne\ell(i)$ for all $i$ \quad(label flip).
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

For the motivating application: the sample contains both $(p,q)$ and $(q,p)$, $\sigma$ is the
swap, the view is a symmetric function of the pair, and the which-factor bit distinguishes the
two orders. Condition (2) is load-bearing --- it is the sample-level replacement for
off-diagonality, a fixed point being a sample $(p,p)$ on which the label cannot flip.

\begin{proposition}[Nonvacuity]
The hypotheses are satisfiable: the two-element sample $\{(p,q),(q,p)\}$ with the constant
view, the label $(\mathtt{false},\mathtt{true})$ and $\sigma$ the transposition satisfies all
four conditions.
\end{proposition}

\subsection{First half: total collision}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:fibertwo}
If $\sigma$ is fixed-point-free and $c\circ\sigma=c$, then $f_{c_i}\ge2$ for every $i$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The fiber over $c_i$ contains $i$ and $\sigma(i)$, which are distinct.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Total collision]\label{thm:totalcoll}
On a swap-closed sample $C(c)=n$: every sample collides and the collision fraction is exactly
$1$.
\end{theorem}

Consequently Theorem~\ref{thm:sandwich} degenerates: for a binary label it reads
$H(\ell)-1\le\Ihat\le H(\ell)$, which is content-free, and Theorem~\ref{thm:powerless} with
$\delta=\log_2|L|$ likewise says nothing. Where the sample is swap-closed, the collision
machinery is vacuous.

\subsection{Second half: the exact zero}

\begin{lemma}[Cell matching]\label{lem:cellmatch}
On a swap-closed sample with binary label, $N_{b,k}=N_{\lnot b,k}$ for every label value $b$
and code $k$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The map $i\mapsto\sigma(i)$ sends $\{i:\ell_i=b,\ c_i=k\}$ into
$\{i:\ell_i=\lnot b,\ c_i=k\}$ --- it preserves the code by (3) and flips the label by (4),
which for a binary alphabet means $\ell(\sigma i)=\lnot b$ --- and it is its own inverse by
(1), hence a bijection.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Even fiber split]\label{cor:even}
$2N_{b,k}=f_k$ for all $b,k$, since $N_{b,k}+N_{\lnot b,k}=f_k$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Balanced label margin]\label{cor:balanced}
Summing Corollary~\ref{cor:even} over $k$ gives $2m_b=n$ for each label value $b$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Product table, hence exact zero]\label{thm:exactzero}
On a swap-closed sample with binary label and $n\ge1$,
\[
m_b\,f_k \;=\; N_{b,k}\,n \qquad\text{for all }b,k,
\]
so the empirical contingency table is exactly a product table and
\[
\Ihat(\ell,c)=0
\]
exactly --- with no null calibration, no sensitivity floor and no asymptotics.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Corollary~\ref{cor:even}, $m_bf_k=m_b(2N_{b,k})=N_{b,k}(2m_b)=N_{b,k}n$ using
Corollary~\ref{cor:balanced}. Dividing by $n^2$ gives $\phat_L(b)\phat_K(k)=\phat(b,k)$, and
Lemma~\ref{lem:zero} applies.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The dichotomy}

\begin{theorem}[Blindness--sparsity dichotomy]\label{thm:dichotomy}
Let $(\ell,c)$ be a swap-closed sample of size $n\ge1$ with binary label. Then simultaneously
\[
\Ihat(\ell,c)=0,\qquad C(c)=n,\qquad \frac{C(c)}{n}\log_2|L|=1 .
\]
The reading is exactly zero and the guaranteed band is the whole of $[H(\ell)-1,H(\ell)]$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Disjointness of the regimes]\label{thm:disjoint}
If $C(c)<n$ --- if the view fails to collide on even one sample --- then no fixed-point-free
permutation leaves the view invariant, so the sample is not swap-closed.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Contrapositive of Theorem~\ref{thm:totalcoll}.
\end{proof}

A sample whose reading is positive, or whose collision fraction is below $1$, is not
swap-closed and its reading is governed by the sandwich; a swap-closed sample reads exactly
$0$ and there the sandwich is vacuous. The two halves of the programme never apply at once.
The $0.97$-bit hint-view reading therefore says nothing about leakage: it was taken in the
regime where, by Theorem~\ref{thm:powerless}, the statistic has no resolution.

\subsection{Why the nulls sat above the data}

Theorem~\ref{thm:exactzero} is about the \emph{sample}, so it transfers to a shuffled
surrogate only if the surrogate preserves the involution --- and a label shuffle does not:
after shuffling, condition (4) generically fails, the surrogate table is no longer a product
table, and its reading drifts upward by ordinary sampling fluctuation.

\begin{principle}
The permutation null of a symmetric view on a swap-closed sample is biased \emph{upward},
away from an exactly-zero observation.
\end{principle}

This is what was measured: observed $0.0000$ against null means $0.1363$, $0.4990$ and
$0.0004$. A screening rule of the form ``flag when observed exceeds null'' is, in this
setting, guaranteed never to fire --- not because there is no leakage to find, but because the
calibration is structurally displaced.

\section{Algorithms}

\textbf{Collision audit.} From a single hash pass over the view ($O(n)$ time, $O(d)$ space)
output $d$, $C(c)$, the sandwich width $\frac{C}{n}\log_2|L|$, the fiber-log width
$\frac1n\sum_{f_k\ge2}f_k\log_2 f_k$ and the maximal fiber size. This is the
\emph{resolution certificate} of any subsequent test.

\textbf{Plug-in reading.} Accumulate $N_{a,k}$, $m_a$, $f_k$ in one pass and sum over nonzero
cells; $O(n+\#\text{cells})$.

\textbf{Permutation null with a guarantee.} Draw $B$ label shuffles, recompute the reading,
report mean, sd and $z$ \emph{together with} the audit width. By
Theorem~\ref{thm:powerless} the whole null and the observed value lie in an interval of that
width.

\textbf{Swap-closure detector.} Match each index $(p,q)$ to the index of $(q,p)$; if the
matching is total and fixed-point-free, verify $c\circ\sigma=c$ and $\ell\circ\sigma\ne\ell$
pointwise. On success Theorem~\ref{thm:exactzero} certifies $\Ihat=0$ exactly without
floating-point arithmetic. Cost $O(n)$.

\textbf{Refinement-gain check.} Compute both readings and the coarse collision term;
Theorem~\ref{thm:gain} bounds the gain, separating ``the hint exceeded what refinement
guarantees'' from ``the hint refined''.

\section{Applications and discussion}

\subsection{Leakage screening} The immediate application is negative and sharp: a
factor-residue hint view carrying $4.56$ of the $4.60$ bits of label entropy is factor-blind
at the sensitivity of a permutation null ($\pm0.001$ bits on this statistic).

\subsection{Beyond factoring} Nothing above is arithmetic. The same phenomena afflict any
pipeline scoring a high-cardinality categorical feature by empirical mutual information
against a label and calibrating by shuffling: leakage audits on hashed identifiers,
``does this embedding encode the protected attribute?'' probes, feature-importance screens on
near-unique keys, privacy audits of quasi-identifiers. Theorem~\ref{thm:injective} says the
score converges to the label entropy as the feature becomes a key, and
Theorem~\ref{thm:powerless} says the shuffle null cannot detect that it has. The actionable
recommendation is one line: report the collision fraction next to every mutual-information
score.

\subsection{Relation to classical bias corrections} The Miller--Madow correction estimates the
plug-in bias as $(\text{cells}-1)/(2n\ln2)$ to first order and is asymptotic.
Theorem~\ref{thm:sandwich} is a non-asymptotic statement of the same phenomenon with an
explicit finite constant, and the two are comparable: for fibers of size at most two,
Theorem~\ref{thm:onebit} gives deficit $\le C/n$ while Miller--Madow predicts
$\approx(n-d)/n$, and $C\le2(n-d)$ places the sandwich within a factor two of it.

\subsection{Limitations} The sandwich is worst-case and one-sided; measured spreads run three
orders of magnitude inside the guarantee. The bounds of Sections 3 and 5 are incomparable and
both are retained. The dichotomy is stated for a binary label and exact swap-closure;
approximate versions require a stability analysis of Theorem~\ref{thm:exactzero}.

\section{Future directions}

\subsection{A permutation-null variance law}
The band bounds the range of the null but not its shape; experimentally the null sd was
$0.0011$--$0.0116$ on bands of width $1$. A label shuffle acts on the fiber partition as a
sampling-without-replacement scheme, so the null variance should depend only on the fiber-size
profile,
\[
\mathrm{Var}\;\approx\;c\,\frac{\sum_k f_k(f_k-1)}{n^2},
\]
with no dependence on the labels beyond their margin. Proposition~\ref{prop:decomp} already
expresses the statistic as a weighted sum of per-fiber entropies, and per-fiber label counts
under a shuffle are exactly multivariate hypergeometric, so the variance calculation is a
finite combinatorial identity rather than an asymptotic claim.

\subsection{Miller--Madow correction as an exact theorem}
Both the collision deficit and the classical correction are now exact finite objects in the
same framework, so a theorem of the form
$|\text{deficit}-\text{Miller--Madow}|\le g(\text{fiber profile})$ is stateable without
probability theory.

\subsection{Strict refinement gain}
Theorem~\ref{thm:dpi} has a strictness witness but no equality criterion. The log-sum
inequality is an equality exactly when the aggregated ratios are constant on each fiber of
$\varphi$, so refinement gains nothing precisely when the label is conditionally independent
of the fine view given the coarse one --- a finite, checkable condition that would turn
monotonicity into a diagnostic: a hint that gains more than the criterion permits is the only
kind of hint that carries evidence.

\subsection{Approximate swap-closure}
Extend Theorem~\ref{thm:exactzero} to samples carrying a partial involution defined on a
$(1-\eta)$ fraction of indices, with a deficit bound degrading gracefully in $\eta$.

\section{Conclusion}

The plug-in mutual information of a label against a fine-grained view is not a measurement of
dependence. Its deficit below the label entropy is a fiber-weighted sum of within-fiber label
entropies, so a view with no collisions reads exactly the label entropy for every label
function whatsoever; with collisions the reading is pinned within $\frac{C}{n}\log_2|L|$ ---
or within $\frac1n\sum f_k\log_2 f_k$, or within one bit for pair-collision views --- of a
quantity depending only on the label margin. The permutation null, preserving both the label
margin and the fibers, is confined to the same band. Refinement monotonicity makes the
apparent superiority of a finer hint view automatic. Against this, the exact-zero side is a
theorem about symmetry: on a sample carrying a fixed-point-free involution along which the
view is constant and the label flips, the contingency table is an exact product table and the
reading is exactly zero --- and the two sides can never be confused, because swap-closure
forces the collision fraction to $1$, which is exactly what makes the band vacuous. A flagged
reading of $0.9663$ bits, matched by its own shuffle null to within $0.0015$ bits, was never
evidence of anything but its own sparsity.

